narrative:
## The Four Ownership Patterns

Every variable in XIOM follows one of four patterns. Knowing which to use is the key to safe code:

### 1. Pass by Value (Move)
```xiom
fn consume_account(acc: Account) -> Float64
  requires: acc.balance >= 0.0;
{
  return acc.balance;
}
```
The caller transfers ownership. After the call, the caller can't use `acc` anymore. Use this when the function should own the data.

### 2. Immutable Borrow (&)
```xiom
fn print_account(acc: &Account)
  requires: acc.balance >= 0.0;
{
  io.println("Balance: " + acc.balance);
}
```
The caller keeps ownership, the function can only read. Use this for queries and displays.

### 3. Mutable Borrow (&mut)
```xiom
fn deposit(acc: &mut Account, amount: Float64)
  requires: amount > 0.0;
  ensures: acc.balance == acc@pre.balance + amount;
{
  acc.balance = acc.balance + amount;
}
```
The caller keeps ownership, the function can read and write. Only one mutable borrow exists at a time.

### 4. Clone
```xiom
fn duplicate_account(acc: &Account) -> Account {
  return acc.clone();
}
```
Creates an independent copy. Both original and clone exist independently. Use when you need shared ownership.

### Putting It Together
Each pattern has a purpose. Move for transfer, `&` for reading, `&mut` for mutation, clone for copying. Contracts add logical guards to each.

